---
.title = "IRC and Rooms",
.description = "Follow an IRC channel from Rooms as if it were a room, or join Rooms from an ordinary IRC client.",
.date = .date("2026-09-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

Rooms and [IRC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRC), the chat protocol that
has run open-source communities since 1988, connect both ways. A bridge
follows an IRC channel and shows
it in Rooms as a room. A gateway lets an ordinary IRC client join rooms on a
Rooms server. Either way, the translation happens at the IRC connection and
nowhere else. Inside Rooms, someone on IRC is a member like anyone else,
with the same rooms, the same lines, and the same direct messages.

# Follow an IRC channel as a room

```sh
zig build rooms-irc-bridge
```

By default this follows Libera's `#ocapn` over TLS and offers it as a room
called `libera/#ocapn` on your machine. The command prints the port and the
room name, and any Rooms client can join. People on the IRC side appear as
`irc/<nick>`, so you can always see where a line came from.

The bridge only reads until a registered nick opts in to two-way mode with
a SASL password. Two-way mode needs TLS, sends at a measured pace, and keeps
only a short backlog across reconnects. A line that may or may not have
reached IRC is refused rather than sent again, so nothing lands on the
channel twice.

You set the bridge up from a form on the server's admin page. A password
goes to the connection that uses it and is kept only so the bridge can log
in again after a restart. It never enters exports, admin screens, or the
server's problem log, and it is forgotten when you remove the bridge.

# Join Rooms from an IRC client

```sh
zig build rooms-irc-front -- --directory=127.0.0.1:9099
```

Point your IRC client at `127.0.0.1:6667`, pick a nickname, and join a
channel named after a room. Channel messages, private messages, `NAMES`,
`LIST`, `PART`, `PING`, `TOPIC`, and `WHOIS` all work. `JOIN` enters a room
that exists on the server, and if there is none, you see the server's
refusal.

Here is one IRC client entering the team room from the
[Rooms]($link.page('rooms')) page, saying one line, and hearing the reply.
Lines marked `>` came from the client, and lines marked `<` came back:

```
> NICK ada
> USER ada 0 * :Ada, from an IRC client
< :rooms.local 001 ada :Welcome to Rooms, ada
< :rooms.local 002 ada :Your server is rooms.local
< :rooms.local 005 ada CASEMAPPING=rfc1459 CHANTYPES=# NICKLEN=28 TOPICLEN=300 :are supported
< :rooms.local 376 ada :End of MOTD
> JOIN #team-guide-20260912
< :ada!rooms@rooms.local JOIN #team-guide-20260912
< :rooms.local 353 ada = #team-guide-20260912 :ada agentic-lead first-impression fresh-eyes mira wire-clarity
< :rooms.local 366 ada #team-guide-20260912 :End of NAMES list
> PRIVMSG #team-guide-20260912 :Reading along from an IRC client. Same room, no browser.
< :mira!rooms@rooms.local PRIVMSG #team-guide-20260912 :@ada welcome. The bridge keeps your place in the room, and everyone else just sees you.
> QUIT :done
```

[The same room seen from a browser. The IRC client joined as an ordinary member named ada, its line arrived like any other, and the member list gives it a message button.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-irc-dark.webp'))

[The same room seen from a browser. The IRC client joined as an ordinary member named ada, its line arrived like any other, and the member list gives it a message button.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-irc-light.webp'))

Each IRC connection gets its own references: to the server, to the rooms it
joined, and to the direct conversations it was introduced into. So a private
message from IRC is a real Rooms direct conversation. It ends at the gateway,
because an IRC client cannot hold a reference itself, and the gateway makes no
claim about who is at the keyboard.

Anything IRC cannot show arrives as a notice instead of half a message:
files, lines too long for IRC, and the buttons that only exist in Rooms.

# One nickname across rooms

One connection to an IRC network owns one nickname. Every channel you follow
on that network sees that nickname, and every change to it. Joining or
leaving a channel changes who is in the channel, not who you are on the
network. The network stays set up when its last channel is removed, and
removing the network takes its channels and its saved settings with it.

Names in Rooms are still just names on one server. The bridge can pair an
IRC nickname with a Rooms member without turning either into an account. A
direct conversation is tied to the registration it was opened with, so
someone who later takes the same nickname does not inherit it.

# What keeps it steady

The gateway allows a limited number of clients, rooms, direct conversations,
and queued lines. The bridge limits how many channels it follows, how much
it buffers, how long it waits to register, how fast it sends, and how it
reconnects. Shutting down waits for work in progress and keeps both the
original problem and anything that went wrong while cleaning up.

The tests open real IRC and glowcap connections, and they run both
directions against a real [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/)
program, including a direct message it answers. The
[IRC README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/irc/README.md)
lists every flag, the ways to deploy it, how IRC maps onto Rooms, and the
limits.
